00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:29.790
So a business may start trying
to do things on their own,

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:31.170
try to deviate from IT.

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:36.330
And so you started getting lots
of discontinuity in services,

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:38.760
being able to maintain
those services, and also

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:41.890
operator scale those.

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:45.040
So technical debt,
as we see it, is

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:47.860
what I was just
defining, the locked

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:50.260
in investments, in
platforms, technology,

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:53.470
development inside those
monolithic applications.

00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:56.530
That's creating not one, a
higher risk of discontinuity.

00:02:56.530 --> 00:02:59.560
So in other words,
more of your systems

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:02.710
are going to be apt to break
that cause outages, cause

00:03:02.710 --> 00:03:05.680
service disruptions, therefore
impeding your ability

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:08.590
to service not only your
enterprise but also your client

00:03:08.590 --> 00:03:09.920
base as well.

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:11.410
You also have
increased complexity.

00:03:11.410 --> 00:03:16.370
So lots of time and investment
into those applications.

00:03:16.370 --> 00:03:18.580
Lots of data, of course,
in there, because that's

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:22.162
where you're housing
all of your information,

00:03:22.162 --> 00:03:24.370
both in your customers, your
products, your services,

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:25.790
and your capabilities.

00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:27.640
So what that's also
doing is that's creating

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:30.820
a lock on your data
and on what you're

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:33.380
trying to be able to carry
the business forward on.

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:37.570
So therefore, what that leads
to is reducing IT's ability

00:03:37.570 --> 00:03:41.560
to be nimble, be agile.

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:44.020
It also affects
the ability of IT

00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:45.860
to be healthy in
respect of being

00:03:45.860 --> 00:03:50.740
able to provide new services,
business continuity,

00:03:50.740 --> 00:03:54.040
constant uptime, but also create
nimbleness and effectiveness

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:57.970
in being able to move the
services and the capabilities

00:03:57.970 --> 00:04:00.505
forward in order to innovate
at the speed at which business

00:04:00.505 --> 00:04:03.400
is looking to operate.

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:07.390
So when we talk about
drivers of app monetization,

00:04:07.390 --> 00:04:10.490
getting ready to
move to cloud, what

00:04:10.490 --> 00:04:12.880
does that whole
aspect look like?

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:14.000
What are we talking about?

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:15.970
So getting ready
to re-architect,

00:04:15.970 --> 00:04:19.480
move to either
containerize and be

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:22.270
more nimble inside
of the back office

00:04:22.270 --> 00:04:25.420
or move to cloud or
even hybrid cloud

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:27.830
as we see most of our
clients doing today.

00:04:27.830 --> 00:04:29.920
So drivers of that app
modernization and cloud

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:33.383
migration is going to
be primarily either kind

00:04:33.383 --> 00:04:34.300
of the obvious, right?

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:36.430
So it's either business
driven, so there's

00:04:36.430 --> 00:04:40.390
business priorities, either
new innovation or revenue.

00:04:40.390 --> 00:04:42.130
There may may be
cost cutting aspects

00:04:42.130 --> 00:04:45.220
as well from the business
side or it can be IT driven,

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:49.810
so whether it's infrastructure
led, technology, legacy,

00:04:49.810 --> 00:04:55.660
end of life systems or drivers
to consolidate platforms,

00:04:55.660 --> 00:05:00.490
licensing costs, other locked
factors as terms of driving up

00:05:00.490 --> 00:05:02.380
the IT budget so
cost reduction being

00:05:02.380 --> 00:05:05.770
a primal importance and then
also the cost of maintenance,

00:05:05.770 --> 00:05:08.920
not just in terms of dollars
but also the resources required

00:05:08.920 --> 00:05:13.360
to maintain those platforms
and infrastructure.

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:17.172
So new approach
needs to be taken.

00:05:17.172 --> 00:05:18.130
How do you get started?

00:05:18.130 --> 00:05:20.620
Where do you-- what is
his journey look like?

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:23.080
So we're trying to
simplify it down,

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:24.450
just three simple aspects.

00:05:24.450 --> 00:05:26.320
So first off, let's
start with assess.

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:30.910
Develop a consistent measured
approach to how you're going

00:05:30.910 --> 00:05:33.440
to remediate, how are you going
to get ready to re-architect

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:35.860
your apps, re-architect
your infrastructure

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:39.610
and your services and be able
to provide that path towards

00:05:39.610 --> 00:05:44.770
elasticity, scalability, and
manageability, essentially--

00:05:44.770 --> 00:05:46.630
then use technology.

00:05:46.630 --> 00:05:49.600
So the best practice,
as we see it

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:51.250
and what we advise our clients--

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:53.200
and also I don't
mean just advise,

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:56.140
I mean, we implement this for
a lot of clients day in and day

00:05:56.140 --> 00:05:56.710
out.

00:05:56.710 --> 00:06:01.650
Digital decoupling. new
way of using an old term.

00:06:01.650 --> 00:06:05.230
And it's my phrase, so I'll
play with a little bit here.

00:06:05.230 --> 00:06:07.540
But-- we've heard
digital decoupling--

00:06:07.540 --> 00:06:09.125
I'm sure many of
you have before--

00:06:09.125 --> 00:06:10.250
what does this really mean?

00:06:10.250 --> 00:06:13.060
So it's separating
out your technology.

00:06:13.060 --> 00:06:15.970
It's providing a
abstraction layer that--

00:06:15.970 --> 00:06:18.310
you don't start from
scratch approach,

00:06:18.310 --> 00:06:20.703
you're laying on top
and with that you're

00:06:20.703 --> 00:06:22.120
providing the
infrastructure to be

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:25.210
able to decouple your services
from your backend platforms

00:06:25.210 --> 00:06:29.530
and enable the separation
from your apps,

00:06:29.530 --> 00:06:34.170
and your systems of record,
and your systems of engagement.

00:06:34.170 --> 00:06:35.720
And then next is
going to be evolve,

00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:38.360
so now it's a matter of-- as you
start laying that foundation,

00:06:38.360 --> 00:06:40.880
you move towards
what technology--

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:44.570
with the decoupling you're going
to move towards a API-centric,

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:46.760
microservices
containers approach

00:06:46.760 --> 00:06:49.407
to all of your applications
and your services.

00:06:49.407 --> 00:06:50.990
Now, take it to the
next step and that

00:06:50.990 --> 00:06:53.258
has evolve to where you're
making it more elastic,

00:06:53.258 --> 00:06:55.550
you're making it to where
you can expand your services,

00:06:55.550 --> 00:06:56.600
your capabilities.

00:06:56.600 --> 00:07:00.260
You've got a framework for
managing, not only security,

00:07:00.260 --> 00:07:03.620
but also managing the
services-- have visibility,

00:07:03.620 --> 00:07:06.890
not only to performance, but
also to what data is being

00:07:06.890 --> 00:07:09.050
passed, how it's being
passed, and being

00:07:09.050 --> 00:07:13.220
able to scale those services,
so that your infrastructure is

00:07:13.220 --> 00:07:17.240
servicing your applications
and not your applications

00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:19.490
being your end customer,
but actually driving

00:07:19.490 --> 00:07:23.830
the use of the data to
enable the business.

00:07:23.830 --> 00:07:28.650
So how do you start
eliminating and preventing

00:07:28.650 --> 00:07:29.910
this technical debt?

00:07:29.910 --> 00:07:32.730
Well, the technical
debt starts, typically,

00:07:32.730 --> 00:07:34.260
all these investments,
as we're just

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:35.850
talked about a minute ago--

00:07:35.850 --> 00:07:39.120
the monolithic application
or applications,

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:43.950
there can be more than
one, so talking about CRM,

00:07:43.950 --> 00:07:48.490
product databases, HR,
numbers of different systems,

00:07:48.490 --> 00:07:51.120
supply chain, you've got
multiple that are typically

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:52.650
existent in every enterprise.

00:07:52.650 --> 00:07:54.840
As we've all
started evolving, we

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:57.720
started with the best
in breed at the time.

00:07:57.720 --> 00:08:00.000
Well, those-- have you--
technology's evolved--

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:03.720
it absolutely evolved and
now there's new stuff out--

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:05.820
rate of change that we
all experience today.

00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:09.000
But what this also means is now
you need to, as you're looking

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:12.330
to re-architect your
applications and enable more

00:08:12.330 --> 00:08:15.300
of a cloud native
ready infrastructure

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:17.160
and your approach
to applications--

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:20.040
now start taking it to where
your decoupling your services

00:08:20.040 --> 00:08:22.170
from those monolithic
applications--

00:08:22.170 --> 00:08:24.960
break out to those services,
break out that data--

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:27.780
and expose it to where you
can actually access it,

00:08:27.780 --> 00:08:30.870
your application developers
are able to incorporate

00:08:30.870 --> 00:08:34.770
those services, consume those
into applications far easier,

00:08:34.770 --> 00:08:37.740
and, therefore, you're able to
create reasonable assets rather

00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:40.650
than constantly creating
the same connection,

00:08:40.650 --> 00:08:44.250
the same interface, the
same API, or microservice

00:08:44.250 --> 00:08:46.510
to a number of
different applications.

00:08:46.510 --> 00:08:49.760
So now you're going towards
developing reusable assets

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:51.260
and then you've got
a basis of where

00:08:51.260 --> 00:08:53.640
you can start
creating scalability

00:08:53.640 --> 00:08:56.950
and be able to
manage that approach.

00:08:56.950 --> 00:08:59.880
So entry points--
where do you start?

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:02.040
Where does this journey begin?

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:03.360
Where do you jump into it?

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:05.340
Every enterprise is
going to be different

00:09:05.340 --> 00:09:07.530
and with that every
application is

00:09:07.530 --> 00:09:10.680
going to be at a different
stage in its lifecycle.

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:13.900
Your business is going to
have a different entry point.

00:09:13.900 --> 00:09:15.180
So where is the business?

00:09:15.180 --> 00:09:16.620
I don't mean IT,
so I'm separating

00:09:16.620 --> 00:09:17.912
those out for a second.

00:09:17.912 --> 00:09:19.620
But IT Is going to
have separate drivers,

00:09:19.620 --> 00:09:24.810
so you need to understand
what are the factors that are

00:09:24.810 --> 00:09:28.890
driving them to want to move
the application or capability

00:09:28.890 --> 00:09:32.370
to cloud in this case, same
thing apply if you're keeping

00:09:32.370 --> 00:09:33.120
it on-prem--

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:35.830
principles are
very, very similar.

00:09:35.830 --> 00:09:38.130
So is it innovation?

00:09:38.130 --> 00:09:40.650
Are they trying to offer new
capabilities, new services?

00:09:40.650 --> 00:09:43.140
Are they trying to provide
business continuity?

00:09:43.140 --> 00:09:46.050
Are they-- is it
revenue impacting

00:09:46.050 --> 00:09:47.920
or is it cost reduction?

00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:49.170
What are the business drivers?

00:09:49.170 --> 00:09:50.753
That's important
factor to understand.

00:09:50.753 --> 00:09:53.480
And then also relate it to it
from the IT side of the house--

00:09:53.480 --> 00:09:55.570
is assess the current
state of the application.

00:09:55.570 --> 00:09:57.030
Where is it at in its maturity?

00:09:57.030 --> 00:09:58.770
Where is it at in its evolution?

00:09:58.770 --> 00:10:02.470
Are-- is IT and business
for that matter cloud ready?

00:10:02.470 --> 00:10:04.530
Do you have those--

00:10:04.530 --> 00:10:08.360
is that support and
infrastructure in place?

00:10:08.360 --> 00:10:11.370
And what are the
priority drivers for--

00:10:11.370 --> 00:10:12.810
from an IT perspective?

00:10:12.810 --> 00:10:13.770
Is a cost reduction?

00:10:13.770 --> 00:10:15.840
Is infrastructure consolidation?

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:18.540
Is it application
renewal, refresh?

00:10:18.540 --> 00:10:21.450
Just understanding those and
then from there you can start

00:10:21.450 --> 00:10:23.598
to begin to figure
out, all right,

00:10:23.598 --> 00:10:25.890
using this framework as you're
seeing in front of you--

00:10:25.890 --> 00:10:27.600
leveraging the aspect--

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:31.560
the concept of decoupling, where
does it begin to fit together?

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:34.650
So, now, you can pick where
is the appropriate entry point

00:10:34.650 --> 00:10:35.710
and you start from there.

00:10:35.710 --> 00:10:40.950
So it's usually understanding
these aspects first,

00:10:40.950 --> 00:10:43.860
knowing that you're going to go
towards a digital decoupling--

00:10:43.860 --> 00:10:47.910
an API, a microservice
containerization approach--

00:10:47.910 --> 00:10:49.050
and then there it starts--

00:10:49.050 --> 00:10:50.980
OK, so now get into
proof of concept.

00:10:50.980 --> 00:10:55.460
Look at your architecture, proof
of concepts on your services,

00:10:55.460 --> 00:10:57.480
make sure you don't
forget about security,

00:10:57.480 --> 00:11:00.750
and also, from a development--
how the services going

00:11:00.750 --> 00:11:03.040
to be accessed and maintained.

00:11:03.040 --> 00:11:04.860
So it's important to
have all four aspects

00:11:04.860 --> 00:11:07.560
incorporated into some of
those proof of concepts

00:11:07.560 --> 00:11:09.780
to validate the
approach, validate

00:11:09.780 --> 00:11:11.880
the business goals
are going to be

00:11:11.880 --> 00:11:14.970
met with the approach
of the architecture

00:11:14.970 --> 00:11:16.360
and the infrastructure.

00:11:16.360 --> 00:11:18.420
And then you start on
your way towards going

00:11:18.420 --> 00:11:20.048
towards implementation.

00:11:22.780 --> 00:11:23.280
All right.

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:24.780
So building blocks.

00:11:24.780 --> 00:11:28.710
So look at change data capture,
so look at how data's captured.

00:11:28.710 --> 00:11:30.368
See how that's
going to play into

00:11:30.368 --> 00:11:32.910
and understand how that's going
to affect your infrastructure

00:11:32.910 --> 00:11:34.410
and your applications.

00:11:34.410 --> 00:11:37.160
Look at your event-driven
architecture.

00:11:37.160 --> 00:11:39.692
How is your-- what
are the events that

00:11:39.692 --> 00:11:40.650
are going to be driven?

00:11:40.650 --> 00:11:42.620
What are the applications
and platforms?

00:11:42.620 --> 00:11:45.148
They're are going to be
driving those events.

00:11:45.148 --> 00:11:46.440
What data needs to be captured?

00:11:46.440 --> 00:11:48.360
How is it going to be processed?

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:50.670
And what is it also
going to be triggering?

00:11:50.670 --> 00:11:53.820
Then you start looking at
microservices and your APIs.

00:11:53.820 --> 00:11:57.750
So what's your API and
microservice strategy

00:11:57.750 --> 00:11:58.680
with Google?

00:11:58.680 --> 00:12:02.010
And what I enjoy working
with very passionately

00:12:02.010 --> 00:12:04.500
for the last five years,
definitely Apigee, and then

00:12:04.500 --> 00:12:08.700
leveraging Istio and Kubernetes.

00:12:08.700 --> 00:12:12.480
Real strong platforms to be able
to springboard off and provide

00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:15.010
this infrastructure
on a robust scale.

00:12:15.010 --> 00:12:17.110
So this is what we do
a lot with our clients.

00:12:17.110 --> 00:12:19.080
And that's ultimately going
to lead to an improved user

00:12:19.080 --> 00:12:19.660
experience.

00:12:19.660 --> 00:12:22.680
So being able to
deliver faster, but also

00:12:22.680 --> 00:12:27.390
improve the user experience to
where they're getting better--

00:12:27.390 --> 00:12:29.490
more value out of
those applications,

00:12:29.490 --> 00:12:32.940
and driving how they're going
to be able to not only use

00:12:32.940 --> 00:12:35.130
the app, but also driving
the adoption of the app

00:12:35.130 --> 00:12:36.910
and the services as well.

00:12:36.910 --> 00:12:37.410
All right.

00:12:37.410 --> 00:12:39.743
So with this, I'm going to
turn it over to my colleague.

00:12:39.743 --> 00:12:41.550
And he'll walk you
through the next steps

00:12:41.550 --> 00:12:44.760
on how you start this journey,
and what's our approach.

00:12:44.760 --> 00:12:47.080
MAMOUN HIRZALLA: Hey,
good morning, everybody.

00:12:47.080 --> 00:12:49.380
So my name is Mamoun Hirzalla.

00:12:49.380 --> 00:12:52.230
My role with
Accenture is primarily

00:12:52.230 --> 00:12:55.020
leading the solution
delivery and architecture

00:12:55.020 --> 00:12:56.340
for North America.

00:12:56.340 --> 00:12:59.800
And I have been in the industry
for more than 20 years,

00:12:59.800 --> 00:13:02.520
primarily building
custom applications

00:13:02.520 --> 00:13:05.340
on different architectural
styles for a variety

00:13:05.340 --> 00:13:06.900
of customers and industries.

00:13:06.900 --> 00:13:09.150
And today, what I'm
going to be going over

00:13:09.150 --> 00:13:12.840
is talk about how we can
take the concepts that Norman

00:13:12.840 --> 00:13:16.800
mentioned, and distill them
into an approach that's

00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:20.730
risk-mitigated, has been worked
out with customers in the past.

00:13:20.730 --> 00:13:23.520
And then what are the things
that you need to watch out for,

00:13:23.520 --> 00:13:26.190
so that when you go implement
this type of journey,

00:13:26.190 --> 00:13:29.160
you are successful and
minimize the amount of risk.

00:13:29.160 --> 00:13:32.460
And hopefully your
C-level suite would

00:13:32.460 --> 00:13:35.890
be impressed by what you have
done as part of the process.

00:13:35.890 --> 00:13:38.970
So let's start talking about
the exponential architecture.

00:13:38.970 --> 00:13:42.840
And what does that mean from
taking an existing monolith

00:13:42.840 --> 00:13:45.000
or legacy type of
system, and trying

00:13:45.000 --> 00:13:47.640
to modernize those
type of systems

00:13:47.640 --> 00:13:51.480
and get them into a shape where
I can fulfill my primary two

00:13:51.480 --> 00:13:53.730
drivers for building
any type of system,

00:13:53.730 --> 00:13:55.860
whether I want to
reduce cost or I

00:13:55.860 --> 00:13:57.690
want to increase
agility and innovation.

00:13:57.690 --> 00:13:58.190
Right?

00:13:58.190 --> 00:13:59.700
So for the past 20
plus years, this

00:13:59.700 --> 00:14:01.595
has been the two
drivers that driving

00:14:01.595 --> 00:14:03.720
the majority of the projects
that I have worked on.

00:14:03.720 --> 00:14:05.260
And this is no different.

00:14:05.260 --> 00:14:06.835
So it's always that
question that we

00:14:06.835 --> 00:14:08.460
have to answer, on
what type of metrics

00:14:08.460 --> 00:14:10.620
that we need to put
upfront to make sure

00:14:10.620 --> 00:14:13.090
that we are successful
as part of the process.

00:14:13.090 --> 00:14:15.940
And as you can imagine
this is not an easy task.

00:14:15.940 --> 00:14:19.170
So in order to get
there safely, you

00:14:19.170 --> 00:14:22.050
have to put some
kind of baby steps

00:14:22.050 --> 00:14:25.500
in place, so that you
can get to that journey

00:14:25.500 --> 00:14:29.730
in a kind of governed
approach, so that you

00:14:29.730 --> 00:14:31.510
can scale later down the road.

00:14:31.510 --> 00:14:33.690
And if you need to
fail, you fail fast,

00:14:33.690 --> 00:14:35.790
as part of agile
type of principles.

00:14:35.790 --> 00:14:38.790
And you adjust
based on the results

00:14:38.790 --> 00:14:41.670
that you have went
through as part

00:14:41.670 --> 00:14:43.750
of the initial steps
of that process.

00:14:43.750 --> 00:14:47.310
And that's exactly what this
number one showing here.

00:14:47.310 --> 00:14:48.960
You need to start small, right?

00:14:48.960 --> 00:14:50.700
And as part of that
process, you need

00:14:50.700 --> 00:14:54.600
to define what we call a proof
of architecture, where you can

00:14:54.600 --> 00:14:56.190
go through these
type of processes,

00:14:56.190 --> 00:15:00.330
and you craft out some use
cases as part of your domain.

00:15:00.330 --> 00:15:02.370
You define one or
two use cases that

00:15:02.370 --> 00:15:04.803
would touch upon
many of the systems

00:15:04.803 --> 00:15:05.970
that you are going to touch.

00:15:05.970 --> 00:15:08.370
So there are no
cutting corners here.

00:15:08.370 --> 00:15:10.738
The reason we cut corners
got us into technical debt,

00:15:10.738 --> 00:15:13.030
and we got into where we are
right now because of that.

00:15:13.030 --> 00:15:15.660
So as part of your POA,
you want to address

00:15:15.660 --> 00:15:18.390
those hard questions,
in order to make sure

00:15:18.390 --> 00:15:20.820
that you don't get
surprises down the road.

00:15:20.820 --> 00:15:22.810
So you try to put to use cases.

00:15:22.810 --> 00:15:26.635
You try to touch upon many of
the layers of the architecture

00:15:26.635 --> 00:15:27.510
that you're building.

00:15:27.510 --> 00:15:28.980
And then you execute.

00:15:28.980 --> 00:15:32.820
And then you figure out what is
the outcome of that execution.

00:15:32.820 --> 00:15:35.490
And what are the things that
went right versus things

00:15:35.490 --> 00:15:36.720
that may have not.

00:15:36.720 --> 00:15:38.520
And what type of
tweaks, enhancements

00:15:38.520 --> 00:15:40.260
that I need to do as
part of the process

00:15:40.260 --> 00:15:44.250
so that I can get to the latter
stages of the process, where

00:15:44.250 --> 00:15:47.010
I can optimize and
scale and deliver

00:15:47.010 --> 00:15:49.920
all of these type of things
with certainty and good results

00:15:49.920 --> 00:15:51.310
as part of the process.

00:15:51.310 --> 00:15:53.850
So that's really what this
thing is all about here,

00:15:53.850 --> 00:15:57.030
to make sure that we get
into these type of steps.

00:15:57.030 --> 00:16:00.420
I've seen many clients in the
past where they go and start

00:16:00.420 --> 00:16:02.370
hesitating, or
things don't go well

00:16:02.370 --> 00:16:04.950
because there are so
many things that they

00:16:04.950 --> 00:16:09.030
needed to iron out before going
and doing starting anything.

00:16:09.030 --> 00:16:10.440
And we've seen that pattern.

00:16:10.440 --> 00:16:13.920
And the way around that
is to define exactly

00:16:13.920 --> 00:16:16.020
what type of objectives
that you want to do.

00:16:16.020 --> 00:16:18.960
And use those guiding
principles to--

00:16:18.960 --> 00:16:21.360
if discussions
happen or conflict

00:16:21.360 --> 00:16:23.850
arises, use these
metrics, principles

00:16:23.850 --> 00:16:25.590
to guide you
through the process,

00:16:25.590 --> 00:16:28.990
and try to make decisions
and move forward.

00:16:28.990 --> 00:16:30.490
And this is what I
mentioned as part

00:16:30.490 --> 00:16:31.698
of the proof of architecture.

00:16:31.698 --> 00:16:33.250
So we select the
business priorities,

00:16:33.250 --> 00:16:35.030
what is it that we
want to accomplish.

00:16:35.030 --> 00:16:37.330
There are definitely
certain business priorities

00:16:37.330 --> 00:16:39.010
that we want to accomplish.

00:16:39.010 --> 00:16:40.630
Pick one or two use case.

00:16:40.630 --> 00:16:43.060
Start with the high level
architectural blueprint.

00:16:43.060 --> 00:16:44.890
If I'm going to
use microservices,

00:16:44.890 --> 00:16:46.750
event-driven type
of architecture,

00:16:46.750 --> 00:16:50.200
what are the primary components,
what are the primary scenarios?

00:16:50.200 --> 00:16:53.410
And then start small in
terms of your DevOps.

00:16:53.410 --> 00:16:55.330
I understand that
when you go and look

00:16:55.330 --> 00:16:58.510
at DevOps type of articles,
you have these 10, 12

00:16:58.510 --> 00:17:02.110
tools that are all integrated
in a nicely mature fashion.

00:17:02.110 --> 00:17:04.839
But is that really what you
want initially from the get go?

00:17:04.839 --> 00:17:05.980
Most likely not.

00:17:05.980 --> 00:17:08.380
I haven't seen one
customer started with that.

00:17:08.380 --> 00:17:11.079
So you need to start with
what makes sense for you.

00:17:11.079 --> 00:17:12.670
One, two tools to start with.

00:17:12.670 --> 00:17:14.109
Doesn't have to be perfect.

00:17:14.109 --> 00:17:16.210
You can start with
that, learn the lessons,

00:17:16.210 --> 00:17:18.280
add additional
tools, find the gaps,

00:17:18.280 --> 00:17:21.970
and incrementally move
over until you kind of get

00:17:21.970 --> 00:17:24.607
to that stage where now
I know exactly what I'm

00:17:24.607 --> 00:17:26.440
trying to accomplish,
I learned the lessons,

00:17:26.440 --> 00:17:29.560
and now I can continue
a cycle of evaluation

00:17:29.560 --> 00:17:33.040
and building till you finalize
that state where you get

00:17:33.040 --> 00:17:35.800
into the optimization mode,
and building at scale,

00:17:35.800 --> 00:17:37.930
and having any factory
or set up that you

00:17:37.930 --> 00:17:39.920
want to do down the road.

00:17:39.920 --> 00:17:41.880
Now, to talk about
digital decoupling

00:17:41.880 --> 00:17:45.360
in terms of more peeling
the layers of onion,

00:17:45.360 --> 00:17:47.400
there are twelve steps
that we have seen

00:17:47.400 --> 00:17:49.440
with working with customers.

00:17:49.440 --> 00:17:51.150
You may merge one
step into the other

00:17:51.150 --> 00:17:52.390
or whatever, doesn't matter.

00:17:52.390 --> 00:17:53.973
But the process stays
the same, right?

00:17:53.973 --> 00:17:56.970
Whether it's 10 or 12, you
have to cover your bases

00:17:56.970 --> 00:18:00.450
as part of what's needed, as
part of the digital decoupling.

00:18:00.450 --> 00:18:02.760
So starting with the
defining the domains,

00:18:02.760 --> 00:18:05.310
trying to understand what
is it that I want to build,

00:18:05.310 --> 00:18:07.260
what type of business
problem that I'm trying

00:18:07.260 --> 00:18:09.270
to tackle, what type of issues.

00:18:09.270 --> 00:18:12.810
All the way down to
decoupling the organization

00:18:12.810 --> 00:18:16.110
and reaching out into a
state where things are

00:18:16.110 --> 00:18:17.860
running in a smooth fashion.

00:18:17.860 --> 00:18:20.242
So let's take step by
step into figuring out

00:18:20.242 --> 00:18:22.200
what these things mean,
and how they figure out

00:18:22.200 --> 00:18:24.385
into digital decoupling.

00:18:24.385 --> 00:18:26.010
So the first thing
I need to understand

00:18:26.010 --> 00:18:27.990
is, what are my logical
boundaries, right?

00:18:27.990 --> 00:18:29.430
If you're an
airline company, you

00:18:29.430 --> 00:18:31.140
have two types of
models potentially.

00:18:31.140 --> 00:18:32.970
You have award booking,
non-award booking.

00:18:32.970 --> 00:18:37.120
If you're a bank, you may
have commercial banking,

00:18:37.120 --> 00:18:38.310
retail banking, right?

00:18:38.310 --> 00:18:38.880
And so on.

00:18:38.880 --> 00:18:41.500
Every customer knows
their primary domains.

00:18:41.500 --> 00:18:43.100
And what am I
trying to focus on?

00:18:43.100 --> 00:18:45.510
I'm trying to focus
on A or B. And then

00:18:45.510 --> 00:18:48.420
within A or B, what are the
priorities that I want to get?

00:18:48.420 --> 00:18:51.000
If I'm having issues
with issuing policies

00:18:51.000 --> 00:18:54.390
for my customers, it's taken
an extraordinary amount of time

00:18:54.390 --> 00:18:56.430
to do that because
of my monolith,

00:18:56.430 --> 00:18:59.400
and then there are certain
rules that nobody knows about--

00:18:59.400 --> 00:19:01.680
is there something that
I can do to fix that?

00:19:01.680 --> 00:19:03.330
Is that part of
my prioritization,

00:19:03.330 --> 00:19:06.730
my scenarios that I want to
tackle as part of the process.

00:19:06.730 --> 00:19:10.110
And then trying to understand
the interdependencies.

00:19:10.110 --> 00:19:12.995
So you probably, at the
beginning of the stage,

00:19:12.995 --> 00:19:15.120
you don't want to pick
something where there are so

00:19:15.120 --> 00:19:18.870
many interdependencies on that
type of particular capability.

00:19:18.870 --> 00:19:21.450
Because that's going to
complicate your life.

00:19:21.450 --> 00:19:24.390
Pick something that is
not necessarily trivial.

00:19:24.390 --> 00:19:27.210
But don't go and pick the
most complex type of scenario

00:19:27.210 --> 00:19:29.360
and say I'm going
to start with that.

00:19:29.360 --> 00:19:31.110
So pick something
somewhere in the middle.

00:19:31.110 --> 00:19:33.570
It touches upon many
aspects of the organization.

00:19:33.570 --> 00:19:35.730
And there is value
associated with that.

00:19:35.730 --> 00:19:39.630
And try to start applying
domain-driven design into kind

00:19:39.630 --> 00:19:41.490
of layering down
what are the things

00:19:41.490 --> 00:19:44.080
that I need to do as
part of the process.

00:19:44.080 --> 00:19:46.320
And then try to understand
those type of things

00:19:46.320 --> 00:19:49.170
that are inter-playing
together to deliver

00:19:49.170 --> 00:19:50.250
that type of capability.

00:19:50.250 --> 00:19:54.810
So that when I go and start
carving out or hollowing out

00:19:54.810 --> 00:19:58.020
that particular monolith, I
understand the ramifications

00:19:58.020 --> 00:20:00.240
of what that is.

00:20:00.240 --> 00:20:02.090
Then we start looking
into the interfaces.

00:20:02.090 --> 00:20:03.900
So as part of the
process, we start

00:20:03.900 --> 00:20:06.690
looking-- if we pick one
domain, let's say it's property,

00:20:06.690 --> 00:20:08.130
and I have different
capabilities

00:20:08.130 --> 00:20:11.400
I want to tackle-- underwriting,
billing, and policy issuance--

00:20:11.400 --> 00:20:12.900
as part of the process, great.

00:20:12.900 --> 00:20:16.202
These are part of your
vocabulary that we want to do.

00:20:16.202 --> 00:20:17.910
And there are certain
things that we use.

00:20:17.910 --> 00:20:19.380
And then we made
the decision, we

00:20:19.380 --> 00:20:21.360
want to be event-driven
architecture.

00:20:21.360 --> 00:20:23.280
We start looking at
what type of events

00:20:23.280 --> 00:20:26.643
that needs to be issued
as part of the process.

00:20:26.643 --> 00:20:27.810
So we're trying to decouple.

00:20:27.810 --> 00:20:30.810
We're trying to put flexible
infrastructures in place

00:20:30.810 --> 00:20:33.390
so that these systems
can inter-communicate

00:20:33.390 --> 00:20:34.380
as part of the process.

00:20:34.380 --> 00:20:37.380
So as part of this,
you start high level

00:20:37.380 --> 00:20:40.710
defining the interfaces,
identifying the microservices.

00:20:40.710 --> 00:20:42.360
And identification
of microservices

00:20:42.360 --> 00:20:45.250
can be another thing that
you may want to consider.

00:20:45.250 --> 00:20:46.750
We'll talk about
it towards the end.

00:20:46.750 --> 00:20:48.990
But at a high level,
you start doing that.

00:20:48.990 --> 00:20:51.120
And as part of
doing that, you also

00:20:51.120 --> 00:20:55.020
start identifying what type of
synchronous, asynchronous type

00:20:55.020 --> 00:20:57.360
of activities that would
need to go with those.

00:20:57.360 --> 00:21:00.040
You may want to look at the
velocity of data changes.

00:21:00.040 --> 00:21:02.795
You may want to look
at how big the data is.

00:21:02.795 --> 00:21:04.170
All of these good
things that you

00:21:04.170 --> 00:21:06.670
need to understand about the
interface would be required.

00:21:06.670 --> 00:21:09.060
And you need to find a way
to start documenting that

00:21:09.060 --> 00:21:11.520
as part of the process.

00:21:11.520 --> 00:21:14.160
And then that's the next
step, which we talk about--

00:21:14.160 --> 00:21:17.360
we now look into the data
in more particular detail.

00:21:17.360 --> 00:21:19.290
Now, it's not
enough I talk about

00:21:19.290 --> 00:21:21.600
these particular attributes
and these microservices.

00:21:21.600 --> 00:21:24.030
I now look at what is
the right structure

00:21:24.030 --> 00:21:25.040
for this type of data.

00:21:25.040 --> 00:21:28.320
Is that a JSON document that
needs to flow between A and B

00:21:28.320 --> 00:21:29.070
systems?

00:21:29.070 --> 00:21:31.560
What is that contract
starts looking at?

00:21:31.560 --> 00:21:36.570
And then we start locking into
what that contract looks like.

00:21:36.570 --> 00:21:38.250
And finalizing those steps.

00:21:38.250 --> 00:21:40.590
And once you finalize
those contracts,

00:21:40.590 --> 00:21:42.900
that's what other
people within the team--

00:21:42.900 --> 00:21:45.630
whether it's offshore
development or whatever it is--

00:21:45.630 --> 00:21:47.700
will need to start looking into.

00:21:47.700 --> 00:21:50.920
And testers will start looking
into what type of testing plans

00:21:50.920 --> 00:21:52.020
they need to create.

00:21:52.020 --> 00:21:53.910
So that becomes part
of what you need

00:21:53.910 --> 00:21:56.340
to start looking into
in terms of governance.

00:21:56.340 --> 00:21:58.620
And making sure
that further changes

00:21:58.620 --> 00:22:00.750
to certain type of
interfaces would

00:22:00.750 --> 00:22:02.770
require some additional step.

00:22:02.770 --> 00:22:05.760
So it's not just let's go
and scratch that field,

00:22:05.760 --> 00:22:09.550
and put something else here
as quickly as we wanted to.

00:22:09.550 --> 00:22:11.580
So that type of
activity would need

00:22:11.580 --> 00:22:14.110
to be checked as
part of the process.

00:22:14.110 --> 00:22:16.350
And then when you start
looking at your services

00:22:16.350 --> 00:22:20.880
and selecting the
right building blocks,

00:22:20.880 --> 00:22:23.130
there are many ways
that you can build this.

00:22:23.130 --> 00:22:25.770
Whether you use the
technology from Google

00:22:25.770 --> 00:22:28.560
or different providers,
each cloud provider

00:22:28.560 --> 00:22:31.470
would give you certain
different type of capabilities.

00:22:31.470 --> 00:22:34.080
We all know probably
Kubernetes is emerging

00:22:34.080 --> 00:22:36.810
to be the global
universal runtime now

00:22:36.810 --> 00:22:40.290
for all of this microservices
type of activities.

00:22:40.290 --> 00:22:45.090
Functions as service is emerging
to be one of those things

00:22:45.090 --> 00:22:48.300
that lots of companies
are discussing, and trying

00:22:48.300 --> 00:22:50.243
to figure out what
they want to do.

00:22:50.243 --> 00:22:52.410
And then there are things
related to infrastructure.

00:22:52.410 --> 00:22:54.450
How you build your
infrastructure

00:22:54.450 --> 00:22:58.950
as part of the process so that
when I go and deploy my code,

00:22:58.950 --> 00:23:01.620
do I expect that
environment to be there?

00:23:01.620 --> 00:23:04.350
Or do I expect to
have scripts that go

00:23:04.350 --> 00:23:05.830
and build that
environment for me?

00:23:05.830 --> 00:23:07.500
And then once I build
that environment,

00:23:07.500 --> 00:23:10.650
that code gets deployed
as part of the process.

00:23:10.650 --> 00:23:12.840
There are different
ways to look at that.

00:23:12.840 --> 00:23:14.490
But these are part
of the decisions

00:23:14.490 --> 00:23:17.250
that you have to make as
part of your organization.

00:23:17.250 --> 00:23:20.070
Some of those decisions
may be appropriate upfront.

00:23:20.070 --> 00:23:22.110
Some of them may become
later down the road,

00:23:22.110 --> 00:23:24.330
as you gain maturity
and appreciate

00:23:24.330 --> 00:23:26.970
the level of complexity that
would be required as part

00:23:26.970 --> 00:23:28.800
of those type of activities.

00:23:28.800 --> 00:23:34.500
And the selection of the right
framework is important as well.

00:23:34.500 --> 00:23:36.690
Some companies may be
familiar with one framework

00:23:36.690 --> 00:23:38.610
versus the other.

00:23:38.610 --> 00:23:40.380
Don't force yourself
into something

00:23:40.380 --> 00:23:42.660
just because company
x is doing it.

00:23:42.660 --> 00:23:44.340
If you have certain
best practices

00:23:44.340 --> 00:23:47.295
within your organization that
you're comfortable, happy with,

00:23:47.295 --> 00:23:48.870
go ahead and use that.

00:23:48.870 --> 00:23:50.760
And everything that
has to be selected

00:23:50.760 --> 00:23:54.540
would need to be
rationalized for the process.

00:23:54.540 --> 00:23:56.730
And then we start
looking at, OK, great,

00:23:56.730 --> 00:23:58.120
I started defining those things.

00:23:58.120 --> 00:24:02.420
Now we look more into
hollowing out the core,

00:24:02.420 --> 00:24:04.590
and primarily using
the Strangler Pattern.

00:24:04.590 --> 00:24:08.270
So trying to be getting things
out until at some point,

00:24:08.270 --> 00:24:12.350
that monolith does not
have any reason to live,

00:24:12.350 --> 00:24:14.070
and we kind of take things out.

00:24:14.070 --> 00:24:16.910
Sometimes you may not
be able to be as lucky

00:24:16.910 --> 00:24:20.030
and completely eliminate that
monolith for various reasons.

00:24:20.030 --> 00:24:22.460
You may have to provide some
access around those type

00:24:22.460 --> 00:24:23.120
of activities.

00:24:23.120 --> 00:24:26.120
But at least you can take
that monolith and hollow out,

00:24:26.120 --> 00:24:29.520
let's say, 70%, 80%, 90%
as part of the process--

00:24:29.520 --> 00:24:31.040
if you're lucky get to 100%--

00:24:31.040 --> 00:24:32.310
and then continue with that.

00:24:32.310 --> 00:24:33.963
So that's important thing.

00:24:33.963 --> 00:24:36.380
And then you start looking at
what type of business logic,

00:24:36.380 --> 00:24:38.390
where would that
need to be existing.

00:24:38.390 --> 00:24:40.490
And what type of
capabilities that we start

00:24:40.490 --> 00:24:42.020
taking out from that monolith.

00:24:42.020 --> 00:24:44.120
And definitely
those are services

00:24:44.120 --> 00:24:46.310
that you have identified
later, and business logic

00:24:46.310 --> 00:24:49.100
that would need to go and
live in those type of services

00:24:49.100 --> 00:24:52.020
that you would need to create.

00:24:52.020 --> 00:24:55.110
And then we look closely
at the events, right?

00:24:55.110 --> 00:24:57.030
So we start looking at
these microservices.

00:24:57.030 --> 00:24:58.290
They're communicating.

00:24:58.290 --> 00:25:00.400
We're decoupling,
we're isolating.

00:25:00.400 --> 00:25:02.490
So what is the pattern
of communication

00:25:02.490 --> 00:25:03.570
between microservices?

00:25:03.570 --> 00:25:05.430
And that's a decision
that you want to make.

00:25:05.430 --> 00:25:08.900
Are you going to allow
your microservices

00:25:08.900 --> 00:25:10.580
to talk to each other?

00:25:10.580 --> 00:25:15.560
And that's a question that
we've seen it full cycle--

00:25:15.560 --> 00:25:17.270
15, 20 years ago.

00:25:17.270 --> 00:25:20.040
Everybody thought that
the criss-crossing mesh

00:25:20.040 --> 00:25:22.440
of everything talking to each
other is not a good idea.

00:25:22.440 --> 00:25:23.750
We created the bus.

00:25:23.750 --> 00:25:25.670
And then when the API
gateway now with service

00:25:25.670 --> 00:25:27.990
meshed, with microservices,
we're saying,

00:25:27.990 --> 00:25:29.930
well, services can
talk to each other.

00:25:29.930 --> 00:25:32.090
It's up to you, everybody
has their comfort zone.

00:25:32.090 --> 00:25:35.780
But if you take that
path to an extreme,

00:25:35.780 --> 00:25:38.810
understanding that
business code the logic,

00:25:38.810 --> 00:25:41.570
if many of these services
start talking to each other,

00:25:41.570 --> 00:25:44.460
you're creating a lot of
spaghetti code down the road.

00:25:44.460 --> 00:25:46.610
So that's just something
that despite what

00:25:46.610 --> 00:25:48.980
everybody says
about this pattern,

00:25:48.980 --> 00:25:50.550
you can use it judiciously.

00:25:50.550 --> 00:25:52.550
But don't go overboard
with allowing

00:25:52.550 --> 00:25:53.930
these things happening.

00:25:53.930 --> 00:25:55.910
And events would be
one way to look at it,

00:25:55.910 --> 00:25:58.190
or isolating the communication
as well, another way.

00:26:00.795 --> 00:26:02.670
And then you want to
look at data application

00:26:02.670 --> 00:26:03.990
and change data capture.

00:26:03.990 --> 00:26:08.370
So when things happen, when
certain state gets updated,

00:26:08.370 --> 00:26:10.590
are there any type
of ramifications

00:26:10.590 --> 00:26:14.450
on other consumers who may
be interested in that state?

00:26:14.450 --> 00:26:16.200
How are you handling
those type of things?

00:26:16.200 --> 00:26:17.940
Are you handling
those through events?

00:26:17.940 --> 00:26:20.160
Or events and a
combination of CDC?

00:26:20.160 --> 00:26:22.530
Or CDC or whatever it
is that you're doing.

00:26:22.530 --> 00:26:26.620
So these are decisions that
you want to talk about.

00:26:26.620 --> 00:26:29.220
And then you start looking
at the variety of the flows

00:26:29.220 --> 00:26:30.310
that you need to have.

00:26:30.310 --> 00:26:33.480
Whether it's sync,
async, fire and forget,

00:26:33.480 --> 00:26:35.330
or events, truly events, right?

00:26:35.330 --> 00:26:36.330
Those needs to be there.

00:26:36.330 --> 00:26:38.040
Not everything will
be event-driven.

00:26:38.040 --> 00:26:39.570
And if you mandate
that everything

00:26:39.570 --> 00:26:41.610
will be event-driven,
you will be disappointed.

00:26:41.610 --> 00:26:45.180
Because there will be a time
where batch or microbatch would

00:26:45.180 --> 00:26:47.260
be required as part
of your architecture.

00:26:47.260 --> 00:26:49.830
So you would have to
have certain provisions

00:26:49.830 --> 00:26:52.860
within your architecture to
go figure out how are you

00:26:52.860 --> 00:26:54.300
going to handle those.

00:26:54.300 --> 00:26:56.820
What does that mean from
security and boundaries

00:26:56.820 --> 00:27:01.380
perspective, whether I want
to enable scenario A or B or C

00:27:01.380 --> 00:27:01.950
or D, right?

00:27:01.950 --> 00:27:05.970
So these are important things
and questions to understand.

00:27:05.970 --> 00:27:09.330
And as Norman mentioned earlier,
the user experience really

00:27:09.330 --> 00:27:10.770
is one of these
things that would

00:27:10.770 --> 00:27:12.900
drive many of those decisions.

00:27:12.900 --> 00:27:16.020
Before we go into any of this
type of digital decoupling,

00:27:16.020 --> 00:27:18.480
the objective is to create
something cool and fancy,

00:27:18.480 --> 00:27:22.210
and it enables me to be more
innovative, agile, et cetera.

00:27:22.210 --> 00:27:25.740
So we typically start
with a very nice idea

00:27:25.740 --> 00:27:28.590
that we employ design thinking
as part of the process.

00:27:28.590 --> 00:27:30.360
And we start ideating.

00:27:30.360 --> 00:27:34.230
And we come up with a
very nice business value

00:27:34.230 --> 00:27:36.670
that would allow me based
on that type of scenario--

00:27:36.670 --> 00:27:39.090
if I carve out a
piece of it, that

00:27:39.090 --> 00:27:42.030
would allow me to be a
differentiated type of offering

00:27:42.030 --> 00:27:45.240
with speed to market against
my competitors, right?

00:27:45.240 --> 00:27:47.713
So let those type of
decisions kind of dictate

00:27:47.713 --> 00:27:49.380
what are the things
that you need to do,

00:27:49.380 --> 00:27:52.230
rather than a blanket
statement saying everything

00:27:52.230 --> 00:27:53.490
is event-driven.

00:27:53.490 --> 00:27:56.640
Because there are tendencies
for companies or organizations

00:27:56.640 --> 00:27:58.477
or humans that--

00:27:58.477 --> 00:28:00.060
the herd mentality,
everybody is doing

00:28:00.060 --> 00:28:02.100
this-- we follow without
necessarily thinking,

00:28:02.100 --> 00:28:04.740
is that the right approach
for me or my organization

00:28:04.740 --> 00:28:07.170
or my type of scenarios
that I want to enable

00:28:07.170 --> 00:28:09.730
as part of the process.

00:28:09.730 --> 00:28:12.780
And one of the important things
that you need to be aware of

00:28:12.780 --> 00:28:17.360
is the rest of the architecture.

00:28:17.360 --> 00:28:21.290
As you probably thinking
in your mind right now,

00:28:21.290 --> 00:28:23.910
it's like, this is not
a trivial endeavor.

00:28:23.910 --> 00:28:28.530
There are many moving parts when
you take an existing monolith

00:28:28.530 --> 00:28:30.090
into a microservice [INAUDIBLE].

00:28:30.090 --> 00:28:31.610
Things were much easier.

00:28:31.610 --> 00:28:35.220
And if anybody says differently,
I would disagree with that.

00:28:35.220 --> 00:28:38.910
Much easier to have one big
Java application or whatever

00:28:38.910 --> 00:28:40.960
it is that everything
happens there,

00:28:40.960 --> 00:28:43.310
I just do one build,
one release, et cetera.

00:28:43.310 --> 00:28:46.933
Not necessarily a good way
to release business value,

00:28:46.933 --> 00:28:47.850
I would give you that.

00:28:47.850 --> 00:28:50.730
But if you were to start looking
at the microservice route

00:28:50.730 --> 00:28:52.410
in all of these
type of moving parts

00:28:52.410 --> 00:28:54.750
that you're introducing
as part of the mix,

00:28:54.750 --> 00:28:57.400
you're adding a lot of
complexity to the question.

00:28:57.400 --> 00:28:59.610
So that's something that
you have to be prepared for.

00:28:59.610 --> 00:29:03.060
Whether that complexity
lies in CI/CD,

00:29:03.060 --> 00:29:05.990
security team structure,
whatever it is.

00:29:05.990 --> 00:29:08.490
The management of the services,
I used to manage one system,

00:29:08.490 --> 00:29:11.250
now I have 20 services--
what do you do with that?

00:29:11.250 --> 00:29:13.530
I had five error codes,
now I potentially

00:29:13.530 --> 00:29:15.780
have 20 error codes.

00:29:15.780 --> 00:29:17.910
All of these things you
have to be thinking about,

00:29:17.910 --> 00:29:19.830
and what does that mean
to your organization

00:29:19.830 --> 00:29:20.940
and your structure.

00:29:20.940 --> 00:29:23.490
And then we get to the
proof of architecture step

00:29:23.490 --> 00:29:25.230
that we talked about earlier.

00:29:25.230 --> 00:29:27.220
Get these two primary
two use cases,

00:29:27.220 --> 00:29:29.640
slice it across many
layers of the system,

00:29:29.640 --> 00:29:31.890
see how this thing would
behave, and make sure

00:29:31.890 --> 00:29:36.570
that you figure out how
your team later on would

00:29:36.570 --> 00:29:39.390
be able to support
this type of model.

00:29:39.390 --> 00:29:42.420
And there is the
next step, where

00:29:42.420 --> 00:29:44.700
we go industrialize,
and making sure

00:29:44.700 --> 00:29:47.760
that we get into automation
and all of these type of things

00:29:47.760 --> 00:29:50.070
once we've mastered the basis.

00:29:50.070 --> 00:29:52.450
And we expand only as necessary.

00:29:52.450 --> 00:29:55.740
So that's important because
many people think you just

00:29:55.740 --> 00:29:59.888
grow the team, add more bodies
to it, I'll be able to scale.

00:29:59.888 --> 00:30:01.430
That doesn't work
with microservices.

00:30:01.430 --> 00:30:05.240
Microservices require those
fully functional teams

00:30:05.240 --> 00:30:09.920
where I want to have people
with different variety of skills

00:30:09.920 --> 00:30:11.402
working as one unit.

00:30:11.402 --> 00:30:12.860
And that has been
the best practice

00:30:12.860 --> 00:30:14.210
for developing microservices.

00:30:14.210 --> 00:30:18.050
Rather than having a team that
does A or B or C independently,

00:30:18.050 --> 00:30:20.190
data, middleware, you name it.

00:30:20.190 --> 00:30:22.940
So you want to make sure
that these things are

00:30:22.940 --> 00:30:24.810
covered as part of the process.

00:30:24.810 --> 00:30:26.807
And then there are
certain kinds of metrics

00:30:26.807 --> 00:30:28.640
in terms of the plumbing--
how much time you

00:30:28.640 --> 00:30:31.400
need to be spending on
plumbing versus other things.

00:30:31.400 --> 00:30:33.950
Plumbing should be
taking more than 20%.

00:30:33.950 --> 00:30:35.690
If you started a
project and you're

00:30:35.690 --> 00:30:38.600
seeing that you're spending
more than 20% of your time

00:30:38.600 --> 00:30:41.870
on lots of plumbing activities,
you're going overboard.

00:30:41.870 --> 00:30:45.230
So make sure that you don't,
as you part of initial plan

00:30:45.230 --> 00:30:47.450
or whatever plans that
you put, making sure

00:30:47.450 --> 00:30:50.570
that those type of things are
covered as part of the process.

00:30:50.570 --> 00:30:53.540
Now, Conway's law-- it's
something that was coined back

00:30:53.540 --> 00:30:55.940
in the 1968 timeframe--

00:30:55.940 --> 00:30:57.740
basically says
organizations tend

00:30:57.740 --> 00:30:59.660
to create functions
or capabilities

00:30:59.660 --> 00:31:02.120
or systems based on
their own infrastructure,

00:31:02.120 --> 00:31:04.940
or their own communication
style org structure.

00:31:04.940 --> 00:31:08.060
So you have websites
matching the structure

00:31:08.060 --> 00:31:09.660
of the organization.

00:31:09.660 --> 00:31:11.390
And that is not
necessarily good for how

00:31:11.390 --> 00:31:12.570
you want to move forward.

00:31:12.570 --> 00:31:16.040
You just need to be aware of
that and that particular bias

00:31:16.040 --> 00:31:19.730
that we have as part of
building systems in the past.

00:31:19.730 --> 00:31:22.910
And my objective
is to move from--

00:31:22.910 --> 00:31:24.140
I was working with a company.

00:31:24.140 --> 00:31:28.100
In their middleware division,
they had 200 people.

00:31:28.100 --> 00:31:30.770
Just 200 people just
to do the middleware.

00:31:30.770 --> 00:31:34.150
Another like 50 or 60
people just to do data.

00:31:34.150 --> 00:31:35.900
Another team doing ETL.

00:31:35.900 --> 00:31:37.790
It's a massive type
of organization,

00:31:37.790 --> 00:31:41.900
with more than probably
like 3,000 people

00:31:41.900 --> 00:31:42.920
in their IT division.

00:31:42.920 --> 00:31:44.270
But that gives you an idea.

00:31:44.270 --> 00:31:47.960
And things moved at a very
snail pace as part of that.

00:31:47.960 --> 00:31:49.700
That will not work
with microservices.

00:31:49.700 --> 00:31:52.670
You want to make sure that
you have nimble teams.

00:31:52.670 --> 00:31:54.890
There are people and brain
trust within the team

00:31:54.890 --> 00:31:56.220
to make decisions.

00:31:56.220 --> 00:31:58.700
There has to be a
common ground somewhere.

00:31:58.700 --> 00:32:00.440
Not everybody goes
doing their own thing.

00:32:00.440 --> 00:32:02.660
But you would need
to make sure that you

00:32:02.660 --> 00:32:03.830
have that common ground.

00:32:03.830 --> 00:32:06.590
And then give the
teams the authority

00:32:06.590 --> 00:32:09.050
and have the skills
within the team

00:32:09.050 --> 00:32:10.850
so that they can
function as a unit that's

00:32:10.850 --> 00:32:12.440
basically effective
and productive

00:32:12.440 --> 00:32:14.370
at the end of the day.

00:32:14.370 --> 00:32:18.090
So let's briefly look at GCP
view and what that means.

00:32:18.090 --> 00:32:20.630
So at Accenture, we've
seen this problem.

00:32:20.630 --> 00:32:22.260
We've seen it many times.

00:32:22.260 --> 00:32:24.080
So we've built some
assets around them,

00:32:24.080 --> 00:32:26.240
and we've built some
logical architecture along

00:32:26.240 --> 00:32:29.000
with additional
physical components that

00:32:29.000 --> 00:32:30.120
goes along with it.

00:32:30.120 --> 00:32:32.000
And that's what you
would see if you were

00:32:32.000 --> 00:32:33.230
to go and build it yourself.

00:32:33.230 --> 00:32:35.600
You would have to go
and figure out, OK, I'm

00:32:35.600 --> 00:32:40.130
doing some type of Event
Hub, which is primarily

00:32:40.130 --> 00:32:43.340
the bread and butter of
these type of systems.

00:32:43.340 --> 00:32:46.220
And then I have to have
certain level of microservices.

00:32:46.220 --> 00:32:49.370
You have to make the decision,
is microservice would

00:32:49.370 --> 00:32:51.230
have its own database.

00:32:51.230 --> 00:32:53.660
Are you going to
share the databases?

00:32:53.660 --> 00:32:55.580
Different pros and cons
of each one of them.

00:32:55.580 --> 00:32:58.310
The school of
thought most likely--

00:32:58.310 --> 00:33:00.770
the tables now are
separate independent, so

00:33:00.770 --> 00:33:02.900
that you can run your
microservices completely

00:33:02.900 --> 00:33:05.180
independent, scale
independently.

00:33:05.180 --> 00:33:09.050
And independence also means
if I deploy that service,

00:33:09.050 --> 00:33:10.792
I will not impact anybody else.

00:33:10.792 --> 00:33:13.250
So these are part of the things
that you need to talk about

00:33:13.250 --> 00:33:15.050
as you identify
microservices, as you

00:33:15.050 --> 00:33:17.930
make these architectural
decisions about microservices.

00:33:17.930 --> 00:33:19.580
And what is a microservice?

00:33:19.580 --> 00:33:21.853
What level of granularity
of a microservice

00:33:21.853 --> 00:33:24.020
that you are going to create
as part of the process.

00:33:24.020 --> 00:33:26.780
For which particular domain,
and what are the objectives.

00:33:26.780 --> 00:33:30.500
So these are things that we
have built some intelligence

00:33:30.500 --> 00:33:31.590
around them.

00:33:31.590 --> 00:33:33.710
And as part of that
exponential architecture,

00:33:33.710 --> 00:33:35.990
we typically see the
pattern of data lake.

00:33:35.990 --> 00:33:38.180
How many of you have
done a data lake

00:33:38.180 --> 00:33:41.510
or trying to implement
a data lake today?

00:33:41.510 --> 00:33:42.332
Lots of hands.

00:33:42.332 --> 00:33:44.540
And those who did not raise
their hands, you will be.

00:33:44.540 --> 00:33:48.500
Because that's a pattern right
now that lots of companies

00:33:48.500 --> 00:33:52.640
are gaining a lot of business
traction value out of that.

00:33:52.640 --> 00:33:55.520
So the analytics, the
AI, the capabilities

00:33:55.520 --> 00:33:57.860
that you can get out of
that, they are significant.

00:33:57.860 --> 00:34:01.520
And those leaders in
the industry know that.

00:34:01.520 --> 00:34:03.080
And they are starting
their journeys

00:34:03.080 --> 00:34:04.155
as part of the data lake.

00:34:04.155 --> 00:34:05.863
So as part of this,
you'll see data lake.

00:34:05.863 --> 00:34:08.420
AI services on top of
that can be invoked

00:34:08.420 --> 00:34:10.550
through serverless
type of capabilities,

00:34:10.550 --> 00:34:13.489
or whatever way you
want them to be invoked.

00:34:13.489 --> 00:34:15.440
And then you have
the gateway on top

00:34:15.440 --> 00:34:18.780
to isolate and govern
many of those aspects.

00:34:18.780 --> 00:34:20.780
And then you have
your consumers up top,

00:34:20.780 --> 00:34:25.340
driven by that user experience
that you created through design

00:34:25.340 --> 00:34:30.070
thinking, and making sure
that I'm going to do that.

00:34:30.070 --> 00:34:34.380
Now, if you translate that
to products and constructs,

00:34:34.380 --> 00:34:35.810
this is how it looks like.

00:34:35.810 --> 00:34:39.320
And by no means this
is a representative

00:34:39.320 --> 00:34:45.620
of a slice of a company
or any type of assets.

00:34:45.620 --> 00:34:49.790
All that this is representing
is these are options.

00:34:49.790 --> 00:34:52.610
So you see that you have
[INAUDIBLE],, Doctor,

00:34:52.610 --> 00:34:58.880
Kubernetes, containers, cloud
run, et cetera, listed there.

00:34:58.880 --> 00:35:00.090
Cloud Functions as well.

00:35:00.090 --> 00:35:02.270
Do I really need to go
and implement all of that?

00:35:02.270 --> 00:35:03.470
The answer is no.

00:35:03.470 --> 00:35:05.840
These are options
for you to consider.

00:35:05.840 --> 00:35:09.890
And each one of those would take
probably a half hour discussion

00:35:09.890 --> 00:35:11.220
to go over the details.

00:35:11.220 --> 00:35:13.820
But I would encourage
each one of you

00:35:13.820 --> 00:35:16.190
to look at what this
thing is all about.

00:35:16.190 --> 00:35:19.850
And then the major question
that also needs to come into

00:35:19.850 --> 00:35:21.230
is the Event Hub.

00:35:21.230 --> 00:35:24.890
Are you part of GCP
going to use Pub/Sub?

00:35:24.890 --> 00:35:27.500
And I put Kafka there
even though it's not GCP,

00:35:27.500 --> 00:35:29.930
but it's coming to
GCP through Confluent

00:35:29.930 --> 00:35:31.250
and potentially other things.

00:35:31.250 --> 00:35:33.190
But Kafka is also is
one of those things

00:35:33.190 --> 00:35:34.940
that you really can't
have that discussion

00:35:34.940 --> 00:35:36.500
without talking about Kafka.

00:35:36.500 --> 00:35:38.390
And then what are
your requirements?

00:35:38.390 --> 00:35:42.680
Things related to indefinite
storage or order of messages.

00:35:42.680 --> 00:35:45.390
Things can be done in GCP not
necessarily out of the box,

00:35:45.390 --> 00:35:47.390
but you can create around them.

00:35:47.390 --> 00:35:49.500
So we can do that, it
doesn't mean that we can't.

00:35:49.500 --> 00:35:51.950
But there are companies who
would prefer certain things

00:35:51.950 --> 00:35:53.360
to be done in a certain way.

00:35:53.360 --> 00:35:55.460
And it would be a mess if
I don't put Kafka there

00:35:55.460 --> 00:35:57.680
as part of the Event Hub.

00:35:57.680 --> 00:35:59.300
And then there are
the things related

00:35:59.300 --> 00:36:01.710
to Cloud Function,
streaming IoT Core, all

00:36:01.710 --> 00:36:03.710
of these type of activities
that you need to do.

00:36:03.710 --> 00:36:05.630
CDC type of
replication-- what do

00:36:05.630 --> 00:36:07.880
you do about them as
part of the process?

00:36:07.880 --> 00:36:09.950
So take that one, look at it.

00:36:09.950 --> 00:36:13.160
And Apigee, obviously, as
part of the API gateway

00:36:13.160 --> 00:36:14.840
is one of these
core components that

00:36:14.840 --> 00:36:16.370
would give you a
lot of flexibility

00:36:16.370 --> 00:36:19.140
as part of your architecture.

00:36:19.140 --> 00:36:21.890
Here's another view of
how that may look like,

00:36:21.890 --> 00:36:25.820
what additional capabilities
flushed out around Apigee

00:36:25.820 --> 00:36:27.370
as part of the process.

00:36:27.370 --> 00:36:32.240
So it's really something
that's not necessarily

00:36:32.240 --> 00:36:35.123
highly different from what
I'd displayed earlier.

00:36:35.123 --> 00:36:36.790
It's just something
that you need to do.

00:36:36.790 --> 00:36:39.860
Firestore is one of
these newer products

00:36:39.860 --> 00:36:44.260
that Google released last year.

00:36:44.260 --> 00:36:46.700
And the model basically
does not dictate

00:36:46.700 --> 00:36:49.550
whether you use database
A or B or C. You

00:36:49.550 --> 00:36:51.380
can use multiple
databases, as you

00:36:51.380 --> 00:36:53.300
can use multiple
languages as part

00:36:53.300 --> 00:36:55.320
of your microservice
implementation.

00:36:55.320 --> 00:36:58.330
So again, what your
requirements are,

00:36:58.330 --> 00:37:01.610
do you use the same database
for the read write of you,

00:37:01.610 --> 00:37:02.840
or something different.

00:37:02.840 --> 00:37:05.812
So it's all depending
on what you want to do.

00:37:05.812 --> 00:37:08.270
And if somebody was wondering
where is the marketing slide,

00:37:08.270 --> 00:37:08.937
this is the one.

00:37:08.937 --> 00:37:11.762
I promise it's only one slide.

00:37:11.762 --> 00:37:13.220
It just basically
shows, we've been

00:37:13.220 --> 00:37:15.770
there, done that, we have lots
of assets and we can help you.

00:37:15.770 --> 00:37:17.360
I'm not going to
expand on it anymore.

00:37:17.360 --> 00:37:20.040
But we've been in the
business for a long time.

00:37:20.040 --> 00:37:22.350
We know what we talk about
when we talk about that.

00:37:22.350 --> 00:37:24.860
So let me briefly
discuss the so what.

00:37:24.860 --> 00:37:27.140
And I touch upon
items of the so what.

00:37:27.140 --> 00:37:29.105
And that's really the--

00:37:29.105 --> 00:37:31.730
if there is the message
that we need to take out,

00:37:31.730 --> 00:37:33.320
it's really considering
the so what.

00:37:33.320 --> 00:37:35.985
What does that mean for
my organization, right?

00:37:35.985 --> 00:37:38.360
There are many things to take
out from this presentation,

00:37:38.360 --> 00:37:40.570
but governance, for example--

00:37:40.570 --> 00:37:43.340
and I had that as a little g,
actually, but somebody updated

00:37:43.340 --> 00:37:44.770
it with a capital G--

00:37:44.770 --> 00:37:45.740
perfectly fine.

00:37:45.740 --> 00:37:47.600
Little g versus
capital G basically

00:37:47.600 --> 00:37:50.900
means I don't want to be the--

00:37:50.900 --> 00:37:54.620
sorry, this is a little
g, just for the record.

00:37:54.620 --> 00:37:57.110
So capital G basically
means you raise

00:37:57.110 --> 00:37:59.990
the stakes of rules,
regulations for everything

00:37:59.990 --> 00:38:01.370
that you want to do.

00:38:01.370 --> 00:38:04.700
Little g basically means
I have rails, guidelines,

00:38:04.700 --> 00:38:07.460
and I empower you to do anything
you want to as long as you

00:38:07.460 --> 00:38:08.540
stay within the rails.

00:38:08.540 --> 00:38:11.000
Just a simple analogy
as much as that.

00:38:11.000 --> 00:38:13.460
So that's a question
that you need

00:38:13.460 --> 00:38:16.480
to think about as you start
creating microservices,

00:38:16.480 --> 00:38:18.290
as you start creating
APIs, how you

00:38:18.290 --> 00:38:20.810
want to govern them,
design time, runtime,

00:38:20.810 --> 00:38:22.280
all of these type of things.

00:38:22.280 --> 00:38:24.140
As you think about
them, design patterns--

00:38:24.140 --> 00:38:27.050
there are many design patterns
that have been established.

00:38:27.050 --> 00:38:28.910
The command query
response separation.

00:38:28.910 --> 00:38:30.980
Reads, writes for microservices.

00:38:30.980 --> 00:38:33.260
SAGA patterns for transactions.

00:38:33.260 --> 00:38:36.320
Multi-phase commit
in microservices.

00:38:36.320 --> 00:38:38.930
So if you have two-phase
commit requirements,

00:38:38.930 --> 00:38:42.410
how can you handle that
as part of microservices.

00:38:42.410 --> 00:38:44.260
Events, compensating events.

00:38:44.260 --> 00:38:46.503
Things that you
need to consider.

00:38:46.503 --> 00:38:47.920
Event sourcing--
is that something

00:38:47.920 --> 00:38:49.630
that you would be
interested in replaying

00:38:49.630 --> 00:38:52.900
type of things, when
state changes and you have

00:38:52.900 --> 00:38:53.680
some discrepancy.

00:38:53.680 --> 00:38:56.530
And I can go and repeat
all of these things

00:38:56.530 --> 00:38:59.200
and recover the state
at a certain point.

00:38:59.200 --> 00:39:00.670
All driven by requirements.

00:39:00.670 --> 00:39:03.520
Don't get into the
mode of yeah, people

00:39:03.520 --> 00:39:04.770
are doing this for everything.

00:39:04.770 --> 00:39:06.145
And therefore, I
need to do this.

00:39:06.145 --> 00:39:06.970
No, absolutely not.

00:39:06.970 --> 00:39:09.137
There are lots of complexities
associated with that.

00:39:09.137 --> 00:39:10.960
And let the
requirements drive it.

00:39:10.960 --> 00:39:12.880
Yes, keep an eye on
future requirements,

00:39:12.880 --> 00:39:15.490
just so we don't
introduce too much rework.

00:39:15.490 --> 00:39:18.670
But let the requirements
drive what is that.

00:39:18.670 --> 00:39:20.800
I talked about service
identification and reuse,

00:39:20.800 --> 00:39:23.340
so that's another thing that
you may want to factor in.

00:39:23.340 --> 00:39:24.940
Yes, independence is great.

00:39:24.940 --> 00:39:26.425
But I want to make
sure that I am

00:39:26.425 --> 00:39:27.800
at the right level
of granularity

00:39:27.800 --> 00:39:29.950
and this is going to
be a reusable service.

00:39:29.950 --> 00:39:32.440
If I'm creating a service
just for the sake of creating

00:39:32.440 --> 00:39:35.710
a service that's going
to be used only once,

00:39:35.710 --> 00:39:40.340
you would have to think about
that as part of the process.

00:39:40.340 --> 00:39:43.710
I want to touch briefly
on the team structure.

00:39:43.710 --> 00:39:45.232
So I talked about it as well.

00:39:45.232 --> 00:39:46.940
But team structure is
one of these things

00:39:46.940 --> 00:39:49.610
that many organizations
think that we can just

00:39:49.610 --> 00:39:54.800
have a microservices team that
handles all microservices.

00:39:54.800 --> 00:39:57.260
That is not necessarily
the right way to do it.

00:39:57.260 --> 00:39:59.780
So you would want to
have the separation

00:39:59.780 --> 00:40:02.810
between different teams.

00:40:02.810 --> 00:40:04.948
You can have multiple teams
handling microservices,

00:40:04.948 --> 00:40:06.740
and you have the brain
trust in these teams

00:40:06.740 --> 00:40:08.660
to be kind of nimble enough.

00:40:08.660 --> 00:40:11.417
It doesn't necessarily
mean one way is right

00:40:11.417 --> 00:40:12.500
or the other way is wrong.

00:40:12.500 --> 00:40:15.170
If you have a microservices
team within your organization

00:40:15.170 --> 00:40:17.990
and that's working for
you, more power to you.

00:40:17.990 --> 00:40:20.673
But if you are starting to
look at it from scratch,

00:40:20.673 --> 00:40:22.340
that would be one of
the things to think

00:40:22.340 --> 00:40:24.260
about as part of the process.

00:40:24.260 --> 00:40:27.067
The networking security
and IAM, that's

00:40:27.067 --> 00:40:29.150
a fundamental question
that you have to go through

00:40:29.150 --> 00:40:31.070
with every GCP implementation.

00:40:31.070 --> 00:40:34.310
You would have to spend a
decent amount of time, typically

00:40:34.310 --> 00:40:36.530
six to eight weeks,
10 weeks, depending

00:40:36.530 --> 00:40:38.600
on your complexity,
the environment,

00:40:38.600 --> 00:40:41.870
to build the environment
in a production grade

00:40:41.870 --> 00:40:43.130
type of fashion.

00:40:43.130 --> 00:40:46.410
But for that POA, it doesn't
have to be that long.

00:40:46.410 --> 00:40:48.200
I can start very
quickly, very fast,

00:40:48.200 --> 00:40:50.060
in about a week, two weeks.

00:40:50.060 --> 00:40:53.260
Put it in a sandbox,
and go quickly on that

00:40:53.260 --> 00:40:56.500
while the other environment is
being built out in a production

00:40:56.500 --> 00:40:57.920
grade type of fashion.

00:40:57.920 --> 00:40:59.600
So do two things
in parallel that

00:40:59.600 --> 00:41:03.290
would save you down the
road as part of the process.

00:41:03.290 --> 00:41:08.110
So with that, I will hand it
back over to my colleague here.

00:41:12.807 --> 00:41:13.890
NORMAN WOODARD: All right.

00:41:13.890 --> 00:41:15.780
So give you a small
little case study,

00:41:15.780 --> 00:41:17.873
examples of some of
the things we did

00:41:17.873 --> 00:41:19.290
and kind of what
the results were.

00:41:19.290 --> 00:41:22.230
I'm not going to go
into it in real detail.

00:41:22.230 --> 00:41:26.610
But basically, rising
costs, compute time,

00:41:26.610 --> 00:41:31.650
need to move migrate to cloud,
more channels being driven,

00:41:31.650 --> 00:41:35.080
have more access channels for
consumers as well as partners.

00:41:35.080 --> 00:41:38.070
So this particular
financial institution really

00:41:38.070 --> 00:41:40.510
was trying to be
very innovative,

00:41:40.510 --> 00:41:42.960
leapfrog ahead of where
the competition was,

00:41:42.960 --> 00:41:46.980
but also really
enable more services

00:41:46.980 --> 00:41:48.390
to get to their end users.

00:41:48.390 --> 00:41:51.870
And also by end users meant
not only consumers, but also

00:41:51.870 --> 00:41:57.060
their customer care, their
data banking, the retail store

00:41:57.060 --> 00:42:00.160
banking, service agents.

00:42:00.160 --> 00:42:03.480
So it was really kind of
a comprehensive program.

00:42:03.480 --> 00:42:06.810
They were stymied by
having, again, monolithic

00:42:06.810 --> 00:42:10.480
applications, monolithic
infrastructure, if you will.

00:42:10.480 --> 00:42:12.740
So in other words,
legacy, very much slow,

00:42:12.740 --> 00:42:15.880
wasn't API-driven, didn't
have reasonable assets,

00:42:15.880 --> 00:42:18.873
everything was very difficult to
normally break out and separate

00:42:18.873 --> 00:42:20.040
from a services perspective.

00:42:20.040 --> 00:42:23.340
But also incorporate
that data and services

00:42:23.340 --> 00:42:27.450
into modern digital
applications.

00:42:27.450 --> 00:42:31.530
So overall, surprise,
surprise, right?

00:42:31.530 --> 00:42:34.110
So their compute time went
down when they moved to cloud.

00:42:34.110 --> 00:42:34.740
Yay.

00:42:34.740 --> 00:42:36.570
There's lots of
good benefits here,

00:42:36.570 --> 00:42:39.233
so I'm not trying to
minimize or belittle that.

00:42:39.233 --> 00:42:40.650
But what I really
want to help you

00:42:40.650 --> 00:42:46.050
focus on is while they became
more nimble in their innovation

00:42:46.050 --> 00:42:48.570
and being able to get services
out to the market faster--

00:42:48.570 --> 00:42:52.030
oh, by the way, that was
typically more than 50%

00:42:52.030 --> 00:42:53.365
That's significant.

00:42:53.365 --> 00:42:55.740
So they reduced their cycle
time for getting new services

00:42:55.740 --> 00:42:57.217
out more than 50%.

00:42:57.217 --> 00:42:59.550
And able to roll out multiple
services, not just one big

00:42:59.550 --> 00:43:00.510
project.

00:43:00.510 --> 00:43:03.210
And oh, by the way, that really
increased the revenue as well

00:43:03.210 --> 00:43:06.450
as their market stance,
market positioning.

00:43:06.450 --> 00:43:08.160
Because now they
were more prominent,

00:43:08.160 --> 00:43:11.170
they would reach more
of their end users.

00:43:11.170 --> 00:43:14.010
And it actually catapulted
them up in their standings

00:43:14.010 --> 00:43:15.490
within their market segment.

00:43:15.490 --> 00:43:18.090
So what else?

00:43:18.090 --> 00:43:19.380
Well, security and fraud.

00:43:19.380 --> 00:43:22.440
They also with the new
services, new capabilities,

00:43:22.440 --> 00:43:27.090
they had additional benefits
of having better security, more

00:43:27.090 --> 00:43:28.980
manageable services,
and better response

00:43:28.980 --> 00:43:32.490
to their security penetration,
and also their security

00:43:32.490 --> 00:43:34.080
architecture.

00:43:34.080 --> 00:43:36.060
Downside that they
didn't anticipate,

00:43:36.060 --> 00:43:38.970
it actually took them
a while internally

00:43:38.970 --> 00:43:43.080
to go through their own internal
security and regulation process

00:43:43.080 --> 00:43:44.928
to break out those monoliths.

00:43:44.928 --> 00:43:47.220
So there were some aspects
that they didn't anticipate.

00:43:47.220 --> 00:43:48.850
They still got business value.

00:43:48.850 --> 00:43:50.550
They still created
that nimbleness,

00:43:50.550 --> 00:43:54.840
they created that decoupling
layer, they used Apigee,

00:43:54.840 --> 00:43:56.530
and they used containers.

00:43:56.530 --> 00:43:58.510
And so they were able to
achieve those results.

00:43:58.510 --> 00:44:00.260
But what they didn't
anticipate is, again,

00:44:00.260 --> 00:44:03.480
looking inside the business,
and aligning business goals,

00:44:03.480 --> 00:44:05.430
business priorities,
and business objectives

00:44:05.430 --> 00:44:09.810
with IT capabilities
and IT priorities,

00:44:09.810 --> 00:44:12.150
so that they were reaching
towards a common goal.

00:44:12.150 --> 00:44:14.990
Hugely important.

00:44:14.990 --> 00:44:18.350
So pulling this
all together, what

00:44:18.350 --> 00:44:22.240
I talked about, what my
colleague Mamoun talked about--

00:44:22.240 --> 00:44:25.460
when it comes to
re-architecting for cloud,

00:44:25.460 --> 00:44:27.340
you've gotten a lot
of information today.

00:44:27.340 --> 00:44:29.270
Hopefully, it's
very useful to you.

00:44:29.270 --> 00:44:32.080
But really, it
comes down to, if we

00:44:32.080 --> 00:44:34.030
were to simplify it,
three different areas.

00:44:34.030 --> 00:44:36.070
So enabling growth and agility.

00:44:36.070 --> 00:44:37.780
Enabling that
innovation and ability

00:44:37.780 --> 00:44:40.240
to introduce new
capabilities, new services

00:44:40.240 --> 00:44:44.670
through differentiated and
modular core capabilities

00:44:44.670 --> 00:44:47.650
that you enable through
cloud-ready infrastructure.

00:44:47.650 --> 00:44:51.360
Also, you want to address
current stability issues.

00:44:51.360 --> 00:44:53.980
It will enable you to address
not only your current business

00:44:53.980 --> 00:44:57.145
continuity plan, but also enable
robust infrastructure to where

00:44:57.145 --> 00:44:59.020
you're going to be able
to continue to launch

00:44:59.020 --> 00:45:01.720
new services without
service disruption,

00:45:01.720 --> 00:45:05.020
and be able to compartmentalize
when you do have any service

00:45:05.020 --> 00:45:07.060
or security impacts,
that you can

00:45:07.060 --> 00:45:12.130
minimize the overall
proliferation

00:45:12.130 --> 00:45:14.320
of those incidents.

00:45:14.320 --> 00:45:17.985
And then, of course, every
IT group and every CIO

00:45:17.985 --> 00:45:20.110
wants to hear, oh, we're
going to reduce costs too.

00:45:20.110 --> 00:45:23.230
But again, it has to be
done in the right way.

00:45:23.230 --> 00:45:26.530
You could end up spending
3x 4x 10x times which

00:45:26.530 --> 00:45:28.900
needs to be done without
the proper planning.

00:45:28.900 --> 00:45:31.390
So the proper planning
is so critical.

00:45:31.390 --> 00:45:33.950
Little g, setting
up the process.

00:45:33.950 --> 00:45:36.460
But with a big G emphasis
on getting it right.

00:45:36.460 --> 00:45:38.750
Do the right planning up front.

00:45:38.750 --> 00:45:41.020
Don't pass that up.

00:45:41.020 --> 00:45:43.810
It is so critical to
have the right plan.

00:45:43.810 --> 00:45:46.090
Figure out what
you need to do, how

00:45:46.090 --> 00:45:49.220
to get and achieve the goals
you're trying to get to.

00:45:49.220 --> 00:45:51.490
But define those
goals more concretely.

00:45:51.490 --> 00:45:55.270
Don't just let a utopian
guidance of a high level,

00:45:55.270 --> 00:45:56.918
I want to go API-centric--

00:45:56.918 --> 00:45:58.210
how are you going to get there?

00:45:58.210 --> 00:45:59.680
What's your microservices plan?

00:45:59.680 --> 00:46:01.150
What's your support plan?

00:46:01.150 --> 00:46:03.790
What's your design
state, your run state?

00:46:03.790 --> 00:46:04.900
How are your data stores?

00:46:04.900 --> 00:46:07.810
What's your service's
continuity plan going to be?

00:46:07.810 --> 00:46:10.450
What is that infrastructure
supposed to look like?

00:46:10.450 --> 00:46:14.590
Set that framework in place
first, define at a high level.

00:46:14.590 --> 00:46:17.440
And then, without going
to the nth degree,

00:46:17.440 --> 00:46:21.620
don't get into
paralysis by analysis.

00:46:21.620 --> 00:46:24.370
But take the baby steps,
and start implementing today

00:46:24.370 --> 00:46:27.550
what you need to, but
plan for tomorrow.

00:46:27.550 --> 00:46:29.660
Key words, all right.

00:46:29.660 --> 00:46:32.440
So wrapping this all
up-- digital decoupling,

00:46:32.440 --> 00:46:34.000
some guiding principles.

00:46:34.000 --> 00:46:34.630
Layering.

00:46:34.630 --> 00:46:39.160
Apply layering to isolate your
systems, isolate your approach.

00:46:41.770 --> 00:46:44.270
Implement appropriate
fragmentation.

00:46:44.270 --> 00:46:46.900
So remove the pieces
that are going

00:46:46.900 --> 00:46:49.700
to cause congestion, cause
conflicts in your architecture,

00:46:49.700 --> 00:46:52.030
in your infrastructure,
in your applications.

00:46:52.030 --> 00:46:54.630
But also in your
governance process too.

00:46:54.630 --> 00:46:57.820
As Mamoun said,
keep it a small g.

00:46:57.820 --> 00:46:59.740
Therefore, you're
driving adoption

00:46:59.740 --> 00:47:04.677
of your infrastructure, your
systems, and your approach

00:47:04.677 --> 00:47:06.760
and tool sets that you're
putting in place for API

00:47:06.760 --> 00:47:08.770
microservices containers.

00:47:08.770 --> 00:47:11.050
Simplify.

00:47:11.050 --> 00:47:12.730
For those of you
engineers, I want

00:47:12.730 --> 00:47:14.530
to keep it simple,
stupid, right?

00:47:14.530 --> 00:47:16.530
Or otherwise.

00:47:16.530 --> 00:47:20.230
And very important--
can't oversimplify that.

00:47:20.230 --> 00:47:21.080
Not to play on it.

00:47:21.080 --> 00:47:25.850
But in order to drive
adoption, enable self-service.

00:47:25.850 --> 00:47:31.405
Enable your business to get
in and develop their own APIs.

00:47:31.405 --> 00:47:34.030
Give them the infrastructure and
the capacity and the tool sets

00:47:34.030 --> 00:47:37.030
and the guidance to have
design standards so you have

00:47:37.030 --> 00:47:38.890
standardization and development.

00:47:38.890 --> 00:47:43.930
But of course, you maintain
some visibility, securability,

00:47:43.930 --> 00:47:46.960
and manageability
of those services.

00:47:46.960 --> 00:47:48.070
Differentiated services.

00:47:48.070 --> 00:47:49.450
Break them out.

00:47:49.450 --> 00:47:51.310
And then automate
those, as well.

00:47:51.310 --> 00:47:52.960
Automate the entire process.

00:47:52.960 --> 00:47:56.890
Incorporate it into your CI/CD
process all the way through.

00:47:56.890 --> 00:48:00.430
And that includes through
even your API platform.

00:48:00.430 --> 00:48:03.580
One of the great things I love
about Apigee as a toolset from

00:48:03.580 --> 00:48:05.560
an API perspective
for implementations--

00:48:05.560 --> 00:48:07.630
because there's so
much that you can do--

00:48:07.630 --> 00:48:09.520
leveraging Kubernetes
and Istio, and then

00:48:09.520 --> 00:48:12.070
bringing it all together in
your CI/CD pipeline with Apigee

00:48:12.070 --> 00:48:15.130
scripted out, to where
you can validate even what

00:48:15.130 --> 00:48:16.870
your security policies are.

00:48:16.870 --> 00:48:18.880
Hugely important, that
automation process.

00:48:18.880 --> 00:48:20.560
It'll save time and money.

00:48:20.560 --> 00:48:23.920
It's not a big investment
from instilling those process

00:48:23.920 --> 00:48:25.300
and developing
those capabilities

00:48:25.300 --> 00:48:26.530
and that automation.

00:48:26.530 --> 00:48:28.540
So don't overlook that.

00:48:28.540 --> 00:48:29.935
Cloud, we all know about.

00:48:29.935 --> 00:48:31.060
Building your intelligence.

00:48:31.060 --> 00:48:34.030
Building your intelligence--
not only monitoring performance,

00:48:34.030 --> 00:48:36.972
but also look at how
your data is being used.

00:48:36.972 --> 00:48:38.680
Look at how your
services are being used.

00:48:38.680 --> 00:48:40.330
Who's requesting what?

00:48:40.330 --> 00:48:41.350
Who are those end users?

00:48:41.350 --> 00:48:44.080
So that you can understand
where your data and the data

00:48:44.080 --> 00:48:46.955
requesting is actually how
that's driving your business.

00:48:46.955 --> 00:48:48.580
A very important
point in understanding

00:48:48.580 --> 00:48:51.130
where you need to
expand and continue

00:48:51.130 --> 00:48:53.350
on your design patterns.

00:48:53.350 --> 00:48:56.123
And then making everything
available in real time.

00:48:56.123 --> 00:48:57.790
That invisibility
just continues to play

00:48:57.790 --> 00:48:59.530
on building to
that intelligence,

00:48:59.530 --> 00:49:02.020
leveraging your cloud, and
bringing it all together.

00:49:02.020 --> 00:49:02.620
Thank you.

00:49:02.620 --> 00:49:04.470
[APPLAUSE]

